INSERT OR IGNORE INTO blog_posts (id,slug,title,dek,description,body_html,topic_key,status,generated_by,published_at) VALUES ('seed-photo-disagreement','why-two-hair-photos-disagree','Why two hair photos can disagree when your hair hasn’t changed','Perspective, brightness, crop and hair state can make the same scalp look surprisingly different.','A practical breakdown of how lighting, camera distance, crop and hair state can distort hair progress photos.','<p>Two photos can look like they document two different scalps even when the underlying hair has barely changed. A few ordinary camera variables are enough: the second image is closer, the exposure is darker, the hair is shorter, or the camera has shifted upward and made the forehead occupy more of the frame.</p><h2>Perspective changes the apparent hairline</h2><p>A phone held closer exaggerates the parts of the frame nearest the lens. That can change how wide the forehead feels and how severe temple recession appears. For a cleaner comparison, use the same camera lens and approximate distance each time.</p><h2>Brightness changes scalp contrast</h2><p>Overhead light can create strong contrast between hair and scalp. Softer light can do the opposite. If the exposure is visibly different, treat subtle density differences with caution. The photo-consistency audit on this site is designed to flag that mismatch before you compare.</p><h2>Hair state changes the geometry</h2><p>Wet hair clumps. Product can create separation. A fresh haircut changes length and direction. Those are real visual changes, but they are not the same thing as biological change in density. Make the hair state repeatable or record what changed.</p><h2>Crop and rotation matter more than they look</h2><p>A slight camera tilt changes where the hairline sits inside the rectangle. When the two frames are not aligned, the eye interprets motion as change. Use fixed landmarks and align the images before inspecting small differences.</p><h2>The practical rule</h2><p>Before deciding that a follow-up looks better or worse, ask whether the images are comparable enough to support that conclusion. Match the setup first. If you cannot, note the mismatch and give that comparison less weight.</p>','photo-comparison','published','editorial-seed','2026-09-09T00:00:00Z');
